// run
// #3068: a generic call inside a generic body whose argument is the
// enclosing generic's own PARAMETER (not a let/for/match/catch-bound local,
// which `rebindCheckerName` rebinds concretely as it lowers). Without
// substituting `genericTypeArgs` through `fc.genEnv`, `resolveCallTarget`
// (compiler/lowerprim.bit) looks up the ledger with the bare, unbound type
// parameter and targets the DEGENERATE instance `f1$0(%0: <T>) <T>` instead
// of the concrete `f1<i64>` `closeInstantiations` already recorded — a
// scalar passed through a slot the descriptor calls a GC reference. `f1`'s
// body still computes the right value even mistyped, so only the emitted
// instance signature (not stdout) exposes the bug; see #2379/#3068 for the
// GC-descriptor consequence and why it does not crash under BIT_GC=stress.
fn f1<T>(x: T): T { return x }
fn build<T>(x: T): T { return f1(x) }

fn main() {
  print("${build(5)}\n")
}
